"""How every state (plus DC and Puerto Rico) sells tax-delinquent property, and who holds the sale.

Each entry is the reference card the dashboard shows for a state and inherits to every county in it.
`system` is one of:
  deed        the buyer gets the property; the owner's right to redeem ends at (or shortly after) the sale
  lien        the buyer gets a lien/certificate earning interest; the owner can redeem for a set period first
  redeemable  the buyer gets a deed, but the owner can buy it back for a set period by paying a penalty
  hybrid      liens first, then a deed sale if nobody redeems (or both kinds depending on the county)

`sale_rule` lets fetch_all.py work out the next statutory sale date for every county in the state:
  monthly_nth_weekday  every month on the n-th weekday (weekday 0 = Monday)
  annual_nth_weekday   once a year, the n-th weekday of `month`
  annual_last_weekday  once a year, the last weekday of `month`
  window               once a year somewhere inside the months listed (no exact day in statute)
  none                 no fixed schedule (sales are set as needed)

`foreclosure` says whether mortgage foreclosures are judicial (through the courts, usually ending in a sheriff's or
court-officer sale) or nonjudicial (trustee / power of sale). `sheriff_sales` says who runs those sales and where.

Figures are from each state's statutes as of 2026 and are meant for orientation, not legal advice. Several states
changed their rules after Tyler v. Hennepin County (2023), so always confirm with the county before bidding.
"""

